<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-first-gdscript">
  <title>First look at GDScript</title>
  <introduction>
  <p>
    This page will give you a brief overview of GDScript. GDScript is a domain-specific language
    for the Godot game engine. This means that its basic functionality is tied to the functionality
    of the Godot game engine, so if a general purpose interpreter were written that was separate from the Godot
    engine then some of the built-in functions of the language would not work by default.
  </p>
  <p>The syntax of GDScript is similar to Python. However, GDScript has variable declaration, and instead
    of type hints, specifying the type in GDScript gives the variable a static type, which allows the
    compiler to check the type before running. This can make finding some bugs much easier, and also
    allows the editor to make suggestions about the available methods on a named variable.
  </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-first-gdscript-hello">
    <title>Hello, GDScript!</title>

    <exercise xml:id="first-gdscript-hello" label="first-gdscript-hello">
            <title>GDScript Intro</title>
            <statement><p>This script currently prints <c>Hello, world!</c>.
                Change the script to print <c>Hello, GDScript!!!</c> 
                for the test to pass. (Note the punctuation.)  
            </p>
            <p>You may also want to experiment with assigning the <c>rotation</c>
                <c>position.x</c>, and <c>position.y</c> properties of the <c>Sprite2D</c>
                to see the Godot icon move and rotate. These properties are also known
                as fields or instance variables, and can be accessed directly using
                the assignment <c>=</c> operator. For example to change the x position
                of the icon set <c>position.x = 100</c>
            </p>
            <program xml:id="gs-hello-gdscript" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-hello-gdscript"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P3.sprite2d_hello_world"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P3.sprite2d_hello_world/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Sprite2D

func _init() -> void:
    print("Hello, world!")

</code>


<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"

func _build_checks() -> void:

   #
   var check_pattern := _add_simple_check(
      "Program should print 'Hello, GDScript!!!",
      func() -> String:
         return (
            ""
            if _is_code_line_match(["print(?Hello,GDScript!!!?)"])
            else "couldn't find a print('Hello, GDScript!!!') call "
         )
   )

</tests>
</program>
</statement>
<hint> <p><c>rotation</c> is in radians. To convert from degrees to radians
    either use the function <c>deg_to_rad(value_in_degrees)</c> or
    multiply by <c>PI/180.0</c> since there are 2 <c>PI</c> radians
    per 360 degrees.
</p>
</hint>
</exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-first-gdscript-process">
    <title>The Game Loop</title>
    <p>
        Godot uses a game loop, so each Godot object, known as a <c>Node</c>
        has functions that are called by the game loop. One of these functions 
        is called <c>_process()</c>

        Here is an example:
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    rotation += PI/2 * delta</code>
          </program>
          <p>
            The game engine does its best to process at a constant refresh rate.
            Typically this is 30 or 60 frames per second (FPS), but it can be faster
            or slower. The <c>delta</c> parameter passed in is the amount of time 
            that has passed since the last time <c>_process</c> was called. So,
            the number that is multiplied by delta is the amount you want the
            value to change each second. In the case of <c>rotation</c> above
            the desired amount is to change by 90 degrees (<c>PI/2</c> radians) 
            clockwise each second.
          </p>
          <p>
            Try adding the code for the <c>_process</c> function above to <xref text="type-global-title" ref="first-gdscript-hello" /> to see the logo
            rotating continuously.
          </p>
  </subsection>
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-first-gdscript">
    <exercise xml:id="exercises-first-gdscript-flipped-icon"
       label="exercises-first-gdscript-flipped-icon">
       <title>Flipping the Icon</title>
       <statement><p>
                Which of the following would cause the icon to be rotated so that it stays upside down.
                (Note: try the code in the <c>_process</c> function of the code in 
                the above checkpoint. Just make sure that there is no other assignment of <c>rotation</c>
                in the <c>_process</c> function, or else you may not see the result.)
       </p>
            </statement>
        <choices  randomize="yes">
            
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <c>rotation = PI</c>
                </statement>
                <feedback><p>
                    Yes! <c>PI</c> radians is 180 degrees.
                </p>
            </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    <c>rotation += PI</c>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                    Look carefully at the operator.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice >
                <statement>
                <p>
                    <c>rotation = deg_to_rad(90)</c>
            </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                <p>
                    <c>deg_to_rad</c> will convert 90 degrees to <c>PI/2</c> radians.
            </p>
        </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                <p>
                    <c>rotation = PI/2</c>
                </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                    360 degrees is 2 <c>PI</c> radians.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                <p>
                    <c>rotation = deg_to_rad(180)</c>
            </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                <p>
                    Yes! <c>deg_to_rad</c> will convert 180 degrees to <c>PI</c> radians.
            </p>
        </feedback>
            </choice>
            
        </choices>
    </exercise>
    <!-- TODO: add more exercises here.-->
  </exercises>
</section>